-\title{Complex Hamiltonian learning and KAM-preserving magnetic control of a catheter model}
+\title{Complex Hamiltonian learning and KAM-guided magnetic control of a catheter model}
 \author{Anonymous authors}
 \begin{abstract}
-A learned model of a magnetic catheter should connect accurate prediction to bounded motion and realizable actuation. We develop this connection for a clamped--free beam in four steps. First, we derive its complex Hamiltonian representation and the complete fourth-order central symplectic update. Second, matched learning and integration comparisons quantify approximation and numerical errors. Third, nonzero-loss, state-only two-mode COMSOL identification yields coefficient and analytic-error bounds that support an a posteriori KAM proof for the fitted Hamiltonian at approximately $1.9$ mm amplitude. Fourth, magnetic force-and-torque feedback realizes that conservative motion in the damped $64$-mode plant. For zero higher-mode startup, the whole-centreline bound is below $1.971$ mm. An input-work constraint additionally guarantees clearance during driven motion, including a quantified nonzero-error envelope over $4$ s. Frequency continuation resolves a resonance-induced clearance boundary, and an S-shaped endpoint task achieves $6.419\,\mu$m RMS tracking error. Classical symplectic and KAM results underpin the analysis; the contributions are the model-specific error bounds, magnetic realization and full-model motion guarantees under stated sensing and actuation assumptions.
+A learned model of a magnetic catheter should connect physical parameters, numerical prediction and realizable actuation. We develop this connection for a planar clamped--free beam while retaining the v10 sequence of mechanical derivation, approximation analysis, KAM theory and magnetic control. Material reference values and magnet specifications define a revised benchmark with explicit design assumptions and endpoint inertia. A common complex Hamiltonian representation supports two routes: compensating dissipation and an identified conservative residual to recover prescribed nominal dynamics, or learning a conservative reference and tracking it with magnetic force and torque. Their exact Hamiltonian reference maps are distinguished from the generally dissipative tracking system. Integral observations identify a restricted analytic residual without acceleration labels. New finite-element calculations, independent integration checks and two-mode control experiments are reported, including five damping scenarios with fresh identification in each case. The quartic magnetic normal form supplies nonzero twist, and deterministic coefficient bounds specify the remaining interface to continuous and discrete KAM theorems. A physical-energy inequality gives a conditional whole-centreline clearance bound. The numerical experiments establish consistency of this minimal computational chain; they do not provide a new interval KAM certificate, a full-network performance comparison or hardware calibration. Parameter-dependent results from the original benchmark require separate regeneration.
 \end{abstract}
