+The two learning routes have different targets but share the same
+mechanical input interface. Route A compensates the learned potential
+residual and damping to approach the nominal Hamiltonian $h_0$; Route B
+tracks a trajectory generated by $\widehat h$. We first realize their
+generalized inputs through a magnetic force and torque, then state the
+conditions for extending a conservative target to all finite-element
+modes. A separate physical-energy argument addresses clearance when
+the requested motion is not confined to an invariant torus.
+
+\subsection{Realizing both routes through magnetic ports}
+\label{app:ports}
+For either $U=U_A$ from \eqref{struct:route-a-control} or $U=U_B$ from
+\eqref{struct:route-b-control}, solve
+\begin{equation}
+ \binom{F}{\tau_B}=A_{\rm mag}^{-1}\Lambda U.
+ \label{app:port-inverse}
+\end{equation}
+Here $\tau_B$ is the active torque, not the sum of that torque and the
+bias-field nonlinear remainder. In the two-mode physical variables,
+the synthetic plant used in Section~\ref{sec:analysis} is
+\begin{align}
+ \ddot a+\alpha\dot a+\Omega_2^2a
+ &=\boldsymbol t E_0(\vartheta-\sin\vartheta)
+       -\Lambda\nabla R_*(X)\nonumber\\
+ &\quad+A_{\rm mag}\binom{F}{\tau_B},
+ \qquad a=D_sX.
+ \label{app:residual-plant}
+\end{align}
+Since $\ddot a=\Lambda Y'$ and
+$\Lambda\nabla V_0=\Omega_2^2a+\boldsymbol tE_0(\sin\vartheta-\vartheta)$,
+substitution recovers the dimensionless plant
+\eqref{struct:plant}. The conversion therefore preserves the actual
+unknown residual; it does not cancel it using simulation truth.
+
+Let the planar dipole be
+$m=\mu(\cos\vartheta,\sin\vartheta,0)^{\mathsf T}$.
+A local field with the required force and torque is
+\begin{align}
+ B(r)&=(B_0,B_y,0)^{\mathsf T}+G(r-r_{\rm tip}),\nonumber\\
+ B_y&=\frac{\tau_B}{\mu\cos\vartheta},\nonumber\\
+ G&=\frac{F}{\mu}
+ \begin{pmatrix}
+ -\sin\vartheta&\cos\vartheta&0\\
+ \cos\vartheta&\sin\vartheta&0\\
+ 0&0&0
+ \end{pmatrix}.
+ \label{app:field-gradient}
+\end{align}
+Direct multiplication gives $Gm=(0,F,0)^{\mathsf T}$ and
+$\|G\|_2=|F|/\mu$. Symmetry and zero trace satisfy the local
+source-free curl and divergence conditions \citep{abbott}.
+The active torque is $\mu B_y\cos\vartheta$; the axial bias torque
+$-\mu B_0\sin\vartheta$ remains in the stored-energy model.
+
+This realization requires $A_{\rm mag}$ to have rank two, a tip-angle
+domain with $|\cos\vartheta|$ bounded away from zero, and a feasible
+joint field--gradient command. The nominal matrix is nonsingular;
+normalizing its columns gives condition number $3.8186$, a geometric
+diagnostic rather than a calibrated actuator margin. In a physical
+installation the map from coil currents to field and gradient must
+also be identified. A single uniform field cannot supply two
+independently specified modal inputs.
+
+The bias and transverse field limits must be combined as vector
+magnitudes. For example, a $30$ mT bias with a $6$ mT transverse
+component requires $30.594$ mT, while a $20$ mT transverse component
+requires $36.056$ mT. The published $40$ mT arbitrary-direction
+capability of the OctoMag apparatus \citep{lussi2021cvs} supports this
+field-strength scale, without establishing simultaneous gradient,
+workspace or bandwidth limits for the present design. The ideal-port
+tests require peak transverse fields of approximately $0.54449$ mT
+for Route A and $2.19794$ mT for Route B. Their command diagnostics
+are reported in Section~\ref{sec:analysis}; no saturation law is
+implemented in those tests.
+
+\subsection{Conditional realization in the complete modal plant}
+\label{app:full-model}
+The two-mode port identity is not itself a truncation-error bound.
+For the nominal finite-element plant \eqref{mech:fe-plant}, retain all
+$N_f=64$ modes and define
+\begin{align}
+ \ddot a_j+\alpha\dot a_j+\omega_j^2a_j&=b_jF+t_jT,
+       \qquad j=1,\ldots,N_f,\nonumber\\
+ T&=E_0(\vartheta-\sin\vartheta)+\tau_B,\nonumber\\
+ \vartheta&=\sum_{j=1}^{N_f}t_ja_j.
+ \label{app:full-modal}
+\end{align}
+The symbol $T$ is a total modal moment, whereas $U$ remains the
+dimensionless generalized input. Equation~\eqref{app:full-modal}
+specifies the full plant for the following result; an arbitrary
+unmodelled force on omitted modes is not included in its hypotheses.
+
+Consider a real analytic target Hamiltonian $h_{\rm tar}(X,Y)$, which may
+contain momentum-dependent terms. With physical low-mode velocity
+$v_{1:2}=\dot a_{1:2}$, infer $Y$ from
+\begin{equation}
+ (h_{\rm tar})_Y(X,Y)=\omega_0^{-1}D_s^{-1}v_{1:2},
+ \qquad X=D_s^{-1}a_{1:2}.
+ \label{app:velocity-inversion}
+\end{equation}
+On a domain with positive definite $(h_{\rm tar})_{YY}$ and convex
+$Y$-fibres this relation is injective in $Y$ and locally invertible.
+The acceleration and port requests that reproduce the canonical target
+are
+\begin{align}
+ a^{\rm des}_{1:2,tt}
+ &=D_s\omega_0^2\bigl[
+     (h_{\rm tar})_{YX}(h_{\rm tar})_Y
+       -(h_{\rm tar})_{YY}(h_{\rm tar})_X\bigr],\nonumber\\
+ \binom{F}{T}
+ &=A_{\rm mag}^{-1}
+   \bigl[a^{\rm des}_{1:2,tt}+\alpha v_{1:2}
+                        +\Omega_2^2a_{1:2}\bigr],\nonumber\\
+ B_y&=\frac{T-E_0(\vartheta-\sin\vartheta)}{\mu\cos\vartheta}.
+ \label{app:full-feedback}
+\end{align}
+The actual full tip angle enters the last line. Substitution first
+enforces the desired low acceleration; differentiating
+\eqref{app:velocity-inversion} then recovers both canonical equations.
+
+\begin{proposition}[Conditional finite-element lift]
+\label{app:conditional-lift}
+Suppose $h_{\rm tar}$ has an analytic embedded two-torus and the
+velocity map in \eqref{app:velocity-inversion} satisfies the stated
+positivity and fibre conditions on a neighbourhood of it. Assume
+$\alpha>0$, $A_{\rm mag}$ is invertible and the exact feedback
+\eqref{app:full-feedback} is available. If the resulting response graph
+lies inside the allowed angle and actuator domain, it is an invariant
+two-torus of \eqref{app:full-modal}. For each fixed low-mode orbit on
+this torus, higher-mode deviations decay exponentially while the
+closed-loop trajectory remains in that domain.
+\end{proposition}
+
+\begin{proof}
+Write the target phase as $\phi\in(\mathbb R/2\pi\mathbb Z)^2$, with
+physical angular frequency $\dot\phi=\Omega$. The demands $F,T$
+depend only on that phase. For the Fourier convention
+$e^{\mathrm i k\cdot\phi}$, the unique bounded higher-mode response is
+\begin{equation}
+ a^*_{j,k}=
+ \frac{b_jF_k+t_jT_k}
+ {\omega_j^2-(k\cdot\Omega)^2+\mathrm i\alpha(k\cdot\Omega)},
+ \qquad j\ge3.
+ \label{app:response-coefficients}
+\end{equation}
+The denominator has no real-frequency zero when $\alpha>0$, and its
+inverse is bounded. Analytic forcing therefore gives an analytic
+response. The velocity map preserves the low torus embedding, and
+differences of higher-mode responses solve stable homogeneous damped
+oscillators. Supplement~\ref{sm:lift} details the construction.
+\end{proof}
+
+This proposition is a conditional statement about the complete
+finite-dimensional plant, not an infinite-dimensional beam theorem.
+It does not attract arbitrary low-mode initial conditions to a chosen
+torus. Route A must additionally remove its non-Hamiltonian damping
+error before the conservative persistence argument applies. Route B
+uses a separate tracking error equation and does not become a
+Hamiltonian closed loop merely because its reference is conservative.
+The present calculations establish neither a new certified target
+torus nor a bound on all omitted-mode startup transients. Establishing
+those hypotheses requires the analytic estimates of
+Section~\ref{sec:kam} and a full-state command-domain calculation for
+the revised parameters.
+
+\subsection{Whole-beam clearance from physical energy}
+\label{app:clearance}
+Let $E(t)=H_0(q,M_f\dot q)$ be the physical energy of
+\eqref{mech:fe-plant}. For every clamped displacement,
+\begin{equation}
+ w(s)=\int_0^s(s-\xi)w_{ss}(\xi)\dd\xi.
+ \label{app:clamped-identity}
+\end{equation}
+Cauchy--Schwarz and the nonnegative kinetic and bias energies give
+\begin{equation}
+ \|w\|_\infty^2\le\frac{2L^3E_{\rm bend}}{3EI}
+                    \le\frac{2L^3E}{3EI},\qquad
+ E_c=\frac{3EIc^2}{2L^3}.
+ \label{app:energy-bound}
+\end{equation}
+Thus $E<E_c$ guarantees clearance of the entire centreline and its
+radius tube in the declared straight corridor. The result holds for
+arbitrary admissible displacement shapes, including all retained modes;
+it is not restricted to a static tip-load shape. For this benchmark,
+$E_c=10.0197171$ nJ and the selected cap
+$E_{\max}=0.95E_c=9.5187312$ nJ.
+
+An input-work governor can enforce this sufficient condition without
+assuming the requested trajectory remains on a torus. Let
+$u=(F,B_y)^{\mathsf T}$ and define the physical power coefficient
+\begin{equation}
+ a_P=\binom{\dot w(L)}{\mu\cos\vartheta\,\dot\vartheta},\qquad
+ W(0)\ge E(0),\quad \dot W=a_P^{\mathsf T}u.
+ \label{app:work-observer}
+\end{equation}
+By \eqref{mech:physical-power}, $E(t)\le W(t)$.
+Given a request $u_{\rm req}$, choose the closest admissible input in
+a scaled positive definite metric $R_u$:
+\begin{align}
+ u=\mathop{\arg\min}_{\widetilde u\in\mathcal U(q)}
+       &\ \tfrac12(\widetilde u-u_{\rm req})^{\mathsf T}
+                 R_u(\widetilde u-u_{\rm req}),\nonumber\\
+ \text{subject to }&\quad
+ a_P^{\mathsf T}\widetilde u\le\kappa(E_{\max}-W),
+ \qquad \kappa>0.
+ \label{app:work-governor}
+\end{align}
+Here $\mathcal U(q)$ is a nonempty closed convex feasible joint port set containing zero active
+input; $R_u$ accounts for the different force and field units.
+If $W(0)<E_{\max}$, zero input is feasible, and
+$E_{\max}-W(t)\ge[E_{\max}-W(0)]e^{-\kappa t}>0$.
+Together with \eqref{app:energy-bound}, this proves clearance for
+solutions satisfying the power identity. Supplement~\ref{sm:work}
+extends the argument to specified sensing and execution-error bounds.
+Additional unmodelled forces require their own power bound in this
+ledger. Activation of the governor can change the requested
+Hamiltonian motion, so the clearance proof is distinct from torus
+preservation.
+
+The reported two-mode tests do not use this governor. Their common
+reference initial state has nominal energy $12.6567$ nJ, already above
+the $9.5187$ nJ cap; Route B starts with an additional position offset.
+Consequently, the observed displacement and slope maxima in
+Section~\ref{sec:analysis} are numerical operating-domain diagnostics,
+not consequences of this energy-cap guarantee. The governor supplies
+a precise condition for a future full-state experiment initialized
+inside the admissible energy set.
