@@ -0,0 +1,51 @@
+Magnetic actuation couples a remotely generated field and its gradient to the torque and force on a magnetic segment. Elasticity, distributed inertia and dissipation then determine the motion of the surrounding flexible body \citep{abbott,kim}. For a device moving inside a confined passage, a useful model must relate these inputs to the motion of the whole centreline. This paper studies that connection through a learned mechanical energy, a geometric numerical method and explicit magnetic ports. The application motivation remains remote manipulation under negligible gravity. The model is an idealized planar beam without contact or surrounding-fluid forces; a clinical device or a terrestrial configuration requires the corresponding loads and calibration data.
+
+The research question has two parts. First, can an identified conservative model define a persistent class of bounded motions and a numerical approximation with controlled structural error? Second, can the damped mechanical plant realize or track those motions through magnetic actuation? The answer depends on which dynamical object is being discussed. A conservative reference, a discrete map and a dissipative tracking system have different invariance properties. We therefore carry one mechanical model through two explicit control routes and state the persistence conditions for the appropriate reference.
+
+Recent continuum-robot studies connect mechanical models directly to magnetic commands. \citet{francescon2025shape} combine a reduced mechanical model with vision feedback for shape-forming control, while \citet{tong2025navigation} use magnetic-rod simulation to support navigation in confined lumens. These developments motivate a complementary analytical question: which properties of an identified energy and its implementation are needed to support persistent reference motion and bounds on the flexible body? The present benchmark isolates that question in a setting where parameters, derivatives and boundary work can all be traced explicitly.
+
+\subsection{Physical parameters as part of the model}
+Variational beam and rod mechanics provide a common starting point for kinetic energy, strain energy and boundary work \citep{simo,antman}. Here a tip magnet adds endpoint inertia and a cosine potential under a static bias field. The quadratic part of that potential changes the modal frequencies, while its higher-order terms supply nonlinear frequency variation. The complete cosine is retained throughout the reduction and control design.
+
+The material input is a literature reference for Sylgard 184 at a stated formulation and curing protocol \citep{cho2021pdms}. Magnet-grade data and a declared bare-core geometry determine a nominal magnetic moment and endpoint inertia. Beam dimensions, bias field and damping scenarios remain explicit design choices. This distinction is essential: a value measured on a published material specimen is evidence for a reference model, while an identified device parameter requires measurements on the actual assembly. Section~\ref{sec:derivation} gives the numerical choices and their provenance; Supplement~\ref{sec:supp-mechanics} provides the dimensional construction.
+
+Changing these inputs changes more than the parameter table. The finite-element eigenproblem, modal-to-physical map, damping normalization and clearance-energy scale must be recomputed together. Consequently, the present results use a single revised parameter record. Earlier finite-amplitude certificates and tracking results belong to their original parameter set until the corresponding computations are regenerated. This revision preserves the v10 organization and its general mechanical arguments while replacing the numerical evidence needed by the present two-route scheme.
+
+\subsection{Energy learning, dissipation and integration}
+Hamiltonian neural networks learn an energy whose derivatives generate a canonical vector field \citep{greydanus}. For actuated mechanical systems, an explicit separation of conservative storage, dissipation and inputs makes the power balance available to both identification and control \citep{zhong,desai}. In this formulation, a complex state packages real modal position and momentum. It does not introduce an additional physical degree of freedom or eliminate damping.
+
+Complex features can couple the real and imaginary channels in a structured architecture \citep{trabelsi}. The energy nevertheless remains real-valued and depends on the complex state and its conjugate. Analytic KAM theory instead uses the holomorphic extension of the underlying real coordinates to a complex neighbourhood. These are distinct uses of complex variables. Likewise, a general approximation theorem does not supply a derivative bound for a particular fitted network: the activation, target class, domain and norm must match the theorem invoked \citep{hornik,zhang}.
+
+The present computational test isolates a restricted analytic feature model. A known magnetic energy is supplemented by one prescribed conservative residual feature and an unknown scalar damping coefficient. Integral observations estimate their two coefficients without differentiating measured velocities. This model is a transparent specialization of the energy-learning framework and permits direct comparison with its known synthetic parameters. It does not constitute a new comparison of full complex and real neural networks. The restriction makes it possible to check the complete chain from the fitted coefficients to a physical magnetic command before enlarging the hypothesis class.
+
+Geometric integration provides a second part of that chain. Classical generating functions yield symplectic maps for Hamiltonian systems \citep{feng,hairer}. A damped vector field does not acquire this property solely by being written in complex coordinates or discretized by a midpoint rule. We derive the proportional-damping Birkhoff representation, identify when compensation restores an autonomous Hamiltonian system, and use one consistent midpoint evaluation of dynamics and feedback. The new experiments use the second-order implicit midpoint method. The original fourth-order central construction is retained as a higher-order theoretical extension with its own assumptions; its old numerical comparisons are not reused.
+
+\subsection{Two routes and the role of KAM theory}
+Route A selects the nominal conservative Hamiltonian as the target. The controller compensates the identified damping and conservative residual. Exact compensation gives an autonomous Hamiltonian closed loop, and its exactly solved midpoint map is exact symplectic. Residual damping changes this conclusion even when its magnitude is small. The analysis therefore distinguishes a Hamiltonian approximation error from a remaining nonconservative force.
+
+Route B uses the learned conservative Hamiltonian to generate the reference motion and adds tracking feedback to the physical plant. KAM analysis, when its hypotheses have been established, applies to that conservative reference or its exact symplectic numerical map. The tracking error has a separate dissipative dynamics. Tracking stability does not make the augmented plant--reference system symplectic, and finite tracking error does not by itself establish an invariant torus of the physical plant.
+
+Classical KAM theory requires nondegeneracy, suitable nonresonance and a sufficiently small perturbation in the specified norm \citep{poschel}. Nonzero training error can be connected to those requirements under additional statistical and regularity hypotheses \citep{chen2022}. For the present restricted feature class, we give a deterministic route from an integral-regression error budget to coefficient and derivative bounds. A small empirical residual alone does not close that budget. An a posteriori approach additionally requires the invariance defect and geometric bounds for a specified torus \citep{haro2019aposteriori}.
+
+The magnetic quartic normal form supplies a computable nondegenerate twist in the revised two-mode model. This is one necessary part of the persistence argument. Continuous and discrete small-divisor conditions, common analytic domains, full remainders and theorem-specific thresholds remain explicit hypotheses. In particular, the new finite-time trajectories and damping scans are numerical evidence for identification and control, not a newly validated infinite-time certificate.
+
+\subsection{Contributions and organization}
+The integrated study makes three model-specific connections. First, it constructs a parameter-traceable beam and magnet benchmark, including endpoint inertia, and derives its complex energy and physical ports in one fixed normalization. Second, it connects integral identification to two distinct control objectives and establishes the appropriate midpoint and tracking identities. Third, it carries the same magnetic nonlinearity into the twist and learning-error calculations, while deriving a physical-energy clearance condition for the complete beam. These constructions use established geometric and KAM theory with the relevant hypotheses retained.
+
+\begin{table*}[t]
+\centering
+\caption{Objects passed through the six-section v10 framework in the integrated revision. Conditional mathematical implications are distinguished from computations completed on the new benchmark.}
+\label{tab:roadmap}
+\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{p{0.15\textwidth}YY}
+\toprule
+Stage & Object constructed & Scope of the result\\
+\midrule
+Derive, Section~\ref{sec:derivation} & Parameter record, physical energy, two-mode coordinates and control routes & Literature-informed material reference; declared geometry and damping scenarios\\
+Evaluate, Section~\ref{sec:analysis} & Recomputed modes, fitted coefficients, independent integration and control errors & Noiseless restricted-feature numerical tests and scenario-specific re-identification\\
+Analyze persistence, Section~\ref{sec:kam} & Magnetic twist, coefficient-to-derivative bounds and KAM hypotheses & Conditional flow/map statements; no new interval torus certificate\\
+Realize, Section~\ref{sec:application} & Force/torque inversion, tracking equation and work-based clearance condition & Ideal local magnetic ports; device limits and energy-governed experiments require additional validation\\
+\bottomrule
+\end{tabularx}
+\end{table*}
+
+Sections~\ref{sec:derivation}--\ref{sec:application} follow this sequence. Section~\ref{sec:conclusion} states the resulting scope. The Supplement contains detailed derivations and a reproduction protocol, with all new numerical quantities linked to the supplied machine-readable results.
