+We have integrated the two control routes into the v10 mechanical, numerical and KAM framework using one revised parameter set. Literature material values, magnet specifications, geometric design choices and uncalibrated damping scenarios are identified separately. Endpoint inertia and the biased modal basis are included in the recomputation, so the mechanical energy, coordinate decoder and magnetic commands share the same inputs.
+
+The two routes connect learning to different mathematical objects. Route A recovers a prescribed nominal conservative motion when the identified residual and dissipation are exactly compensated. Route B generates a learned conservative reference and stabilizes a separate tracking error. The conservative midpoint map has an exact symplectic interpretation under its stated hypotheses; a remaining dissipative error is retained in the physical closed loop. The magnetic quartic normal form provides nonzero twist, and integral-regression estimates specify how a validated coefficient error could enter a KAM theorem. This establishes a conditional analytical connection rather than a new finite-amplitude torus certificate.
+
+The recomputed finite-element frequencies, independent integration checks and minimal two-mode experiments are consistent with that construction. Damping sensitivity is assessed by re-identifying each scenario, and all trajectory results retain their noiseless synthetic scope. A separate physical-energy inequality yields a sufficient whole-centreline clearance condition. The reported control runs do not impose that energy budget and therefore are not evidence of energy-governed clearance.
+
+The next validation requirements are now concrete: identify the assembled specimen and its damping, calibrate joint field--gradient actuation and sensing errors, test nonlinear modal truncation against an independent full model, and recompute theorem-specific analytic and interval bounds on a declared domain. Full-network comparisons, the original COMSOL studies and long-time certificates require regeneration under the revised parameters before their conclusions can be transferred. The present manuscript and its reproducibility files provide a consistent basis for that extension.
