+\subsection{From nonzero integral loss to a controlled perturbation}
+\label{kam:learning-section}
+
+The connection to KAM theory requires a bound on a Hamiltonian over a neighborhood, rather than agreement along a finite collection of trajectories. The nonzero-loss viewpoint of \citet{chen2022} motivates this connection. For the finite readout used here, it can be made deterministic and explicit, without assuming that a training residual vanishes or treating a real-domain prediction error as an analytic norm.
+
+Let $D,y$ be the stacked quantities in \eqref{struct:integral-design}, $\theta_*=(\beta_*^{\mathsf T},\gamma)^{\mathsf T}$ and $e=y-D\theta_*$. Define
+\begin{equation}\label{kam:gram}
+ G_I=\frac{D^{\mathsf T}D}{nL_I},\qquad
+ r_I=\frac{\norm{D\widehat\theta-y}_2}{\sqrt{nL_I}}.
+\end{equation}
+The defect $e$ includes observation reconstruction, input calibration, quadrature and model-class error. Suppose independent estimates give
+$\norm e_2/\sqrt{nL_I}\le\epsilon_I$ and
+$\lambda_{\min}(G_I)\ge\underline\lambda>0$ for the matrices actually used. Then
+\begin{equation}\label{kam:integral-bound}
+ \norm{\widehat\theta-\theta_*}_2
+ \le b_I:=\frac{r_I+\epsilon_I}{\sqrt{\underline\lambda}}.
+\end{equation}
+Indeed, $D(\widehat\theta-\theta_*)=(D\widehat\theta-y)+e$, and the least singular value of $D/\sqrt{nL_I}$ bounds its left-hand side below. This argument applies to any fitted coefficients, including a constrained damping estimate. More observations help only insofar as they improve the defect or the excitation represented by $G_I$.
+
+Let $\mathcal U\subset\C^{2n}$ be a specified complex neighborhood of the real working domain $K$, and define
+\begin{equation}\label{kam:feature-bounds}
+\begin{aligned}
+ B_F(\mathcal U)&=\left(\sum_j\norm{F_j}_{\mathcal U}^{2}\right)^{1/2},\\
+ B_{\nabla F}(K)&=\left(\sum_j\sup_K\norm{\nabla F_j}_2^2\right)^{1/2}.
+\end{aligned}
+\end{equation}
+If $R_*=\sum_j\beta_{*,j}F_j+R_{\rm out}$, Cauchy--Schwarz yields
+\begin{equation}\label{kam:analytic-error}
+\begin{aligned}
+ \norm{\delta R}_{\mathcal U}
+    &\le b_I B_F(\mathcal U)+\norm{R_{\rm out}}_{\mathcal U},\\
+ \sup_K\norm{\nabla\delta R}_2
+    &\le b_I B_{\nabla F}(K)+\sup_K\norm{\nabla R_{\rm out}}_2,\\
+ |\delta\gamma|&\le b_I.
+\end{aligned}
+\end{equation}
+The first line is an energy perturbation budget for KAM, whereas the second and third enter the tracking equation. Bounds on derivatives over a smaller complex domain also follow from Cauchy estimates, with explicit dependence on the lost domain width. An independently controlled $R_{\rm out}$ must enter both the observation defect and these function bounds. For the synthetic single-feature plant it is zero. The numerical truth-based defect reported in Section~\ref{sec:analysis} is a diagnostic of that experiment; a rigorous or experimental certificate additionally needs validated uncertainty bounds.
+
+\subsection{Real analyticity and the correct complex extension}
+\label{kam:analyticity}
+
+The use of complex state coordinates does not by itself supply the analyticity hypothesis. The energy is real on $(X,Y)\in\R^{2n}$, and its extension is holomorphic in the $2n$ independent complex variables $(X,Y)$, or equivalently in independent $(\zeta,\chi)$. On the physical slice $\chi=\bar\zeta$, but this conjugacy is not imposed while taking derivatives in the extended domain. For example,
+\begin{equation}\label{kam:complex-energy}
+\begin{aligned}
+ h_0^{\C}(\zeta,\chi)
+  ={}&\sum_j\nu_j\zeta_j\chi_j
+      +1-\cos\!\left(\frac{d^{\mathsf T}(\zeta+\chi)}{\sqrt2}\right)\\
+     &-\frac{[d^{\mathsf T}(\zeta+\chi)]^2}{4}.
+\end{aligned}
+\end{equation}
+This is the extension of the physical real energy, not a holomorphic function of $\zeta$ alone. Its physical Hamilton equations are obtained by restricting
+$\zeta'=-\mathrm i h_\chi^{\C}$ and
+$\chi'=\mathrm i h_\zeta^{\C}$ to the real slice.
+
+For a neural readout, every operation must possess a holomorphic continuation on the chosen domain. Polynomial and cosine features meet this requirement. Meromorphic activations require a domain avoiding their poles; piecewise-linear activations do not satisfy this analytic version of KAM. A real part taken at the output is continued by conjugate-symmetric coefficients in the independent variables, not by applying the nonholomorphic real-part operation inside the complex domain. Supplement~\ref{ss:analytic-extension} makes this construction explicit. Both the true residual and the learned residual need the stated regularity. A finitely differentiable KAM theorem is a separate alternative with different differentiability and norm hypotheses; it is not invoked for the present benchmark.
+
+\subsection{A genuine local action--angle model and its twist}
+\label{kam:normalform-section}
+
+The balanced variables already diagonalize the positive quadratic energy, $h_2=\tfrac12\sum_j\nu_j(X_j^2+Y_j^2)$. The polar coordinates
+$X_j=\sqrt{2I_j}\cos\phi_j$,
+$Y_j=-\sqrt{2I_j}\sin\phi_j$
+are canonical and give $h_2=\nu\cdot I$. Their radius $I_j$ is not an exact action of the coupled cosine Hamiltonian. A near-identity canonical normal-form transformation is needed before separating an integrable part from a remainder.
+
+For the scalar family $h_\kappa=h_2+\kappa g(d^{\mathsf T}X)$, the quartic term is $h_{4,\kappa}=-\kappa(d^{\mathsf T}X)^4/24$. Assume
+$k\cdot\nu\ne0$ for $0<|k|_1\le4$. The quartic homological equation
+\begin{equation}\label{kam:homological}
+ \{h_2,W_{4,\kappa}\}
+    =-(h_{4,\kappa}-\langle h_{4,\kappa}\rangle)
+\end{equation}
+has a polynomial solution; the bracket convention is
+$\{f,g\}=\nabla f^{\mathsf T}J\nabla g$.
+Its local Hamiltonian flow, followed by the polar parametrization on positive actions, defines a canonical chart $\mathcal T_\kappa$. Direct angle averaging gives
+\begin{equation}\label{kam:magnetic-twist}
+\begin{aligned}
+ h_\kappa\circ\mathcal T_\kappa
+       &=H_{\rm NF}^{\kappa}(I)+R_6^\kappa(I,\phi),\\
+ H_{\rm NF}^{\kappa}(I)
+       &=\nu\cdot I+\tfrac12I^{\mathsf T}A_\kappa I,\\
+ A_\kappa&=-\frac{\kappa}{8}
+ \begin{pmatrix}
+ d_1^4&2d_1^2d_2^2\\
+ 2d_1^2d_2^2&d_2^4
+ \end{pmatrix},\\
+ \det A_\kappa&=-\frac{3\kappa^2d_1^4d_2^4}{64}.
+\end{aligned}
+\end{equation}
+The off-diagonal terms are essential. The twist is indefinite and nonsingular when $\kappa d_1d_2\ne0$; Kolmogorov nondegeneracy requires invertibility, not positive definiteness. For the new baseline, direct floating-point evaluation gives
+$\det A_1\simeq-1.719\times10^{-5}$ and
+$\sigma_{\min}(A_1)\simeq2.065\times10^{-3}$. These are reproducible algebraic checks, not interval bounds for a torus. The finite nonresonance check through degree four is also distinct from the all-integer small-divisor condition below.
+
+The remainder is sixth order in Cartesian amplitude because the energy and quartic transformation are even. It includes both the original cosine tail and Poisson-bracket terms generated by the transformation. In particular, $W_{4,\kappa}=\kappa W_{4,1}$ produces terms proportional to $\kappa^2$, so the complete remainder cannot be obtained by multiplying a nominal tail estimate by $\kappa$. Supplement~\ref{ss:normalform} gives this construction and its extension when cubic terms are present.
+
+\subsection{Separate perturbation budgets for the two routes}
+\label{kam:budgets-section}
+
+For Route~A, choose the nominal chart $\mathcal T_A=\mathcal T_1$. For Route~B in the single-feature model, choose $\mathcal T_B=\mathcal T_{\kappa_*}$ with $\kappa_*=1+\beta_*$. A broader true residual requires a corresponding normal form, or inclusion of its additional terms in the perturbation budget. For the single-feature plant, the energies relevant to the two claims satisfy
+\begin{equation}\label{kam:route-budgets}
+\begin{aligned}
+ h_c\circ\mathcal T_A
+   &=H_{\rm NF}^{1}+R_6^{1}+\delta R\circ\mathcal T_A,\\
+ \widehat h\circ\mathcal T_B
+   &=H_{\rm NF}^{\kappa_*}+R_6^{\kappa_*}
+                                      -\delta R\circ\mathcal T_B,\\
+ \epsilon_A&=\norm{R_6^{1}}_{\mathcal D_A}+b_R^A,\\
+ \epsilon_B&=\norm{R_6^{\kappa_*}}_{\mathcal D_B}+b_R^B.
+\end{aligned}
+\end{equation}
+Here $b_R^s$ bounds $\delta R\circ\mathcal T_s$ on the declared complex action--angle domain $\mathcal D_s$. A common coefficient bound can be reused, but a common analytic function bound is valid only if both chart images lie inside the same controlled physical complex neighborhood. The two normal forms generally have different frequencies, remainders and admissible action ranges.
+
+For the synthetic plant $\kappa_*=1.2$. For an unknown plant, \eqref{kam:integral-bound} gives a parameter interval about $\widehat\beta$; the twist and remainder estimates must hold uniformly over the asserted interval, including exclusion of $\kappa_*=0$. Alternatively, the frozen learned Hamiltonian can be put directly into normal form and certified on its own. Neither procedure identifies a physical invariant torus from a training loss alone.
+
+\begin{proposition}[Conditional persistence for the continuous models]
+\label{kam:flow-theorem}
+For either route, let $\mathcal T_s$ be analytic and canonical on a complex neighborhood $\mathcal D_s$ of $\mathcal I_s\times\T^2$, with $\mathcal I_s\Subset(0,\infty)^2$. Assume that its real image lies in the modeled working domain, that the integrable frequency map $\omega_s=\nabla_IH_{\rm NF}^s$ is nondegenerate there, and that all the domain and norm bounds in \eqref{kam:route-budgets} are valid. Select an interior frequency set satisfying
+\begin{equation}\label{kam:flow-divisors}
+ |k\cdot\omega|\ge\gamma_D|k|_1^{-\tau_D},
+ \quad k\in\mathbb Z^2\setminus\{0\},\quad\tau_D>1.
+\end{equation}
+If $\epsilon_s$ is below the analytic KAM threshold for these domains, twist bounds and Diophantine parameters, the corresponding Hamiltonian has a Cantor family of invariant Lagrangian tori carrying quasiperiodic flow. A positive-measure family follows for the interior domains and sufficiently small exclusion parameter in the classical theorem. For Route~A this is a statement about the actual closed loop only under the conservative realization premise following \eqref{struct:route-a-closed}; for Route~B it is a statement about the learned reference.
+\end{proposition}
+
+The proposition follows by substituting \eqref{kam:route-budgets} into the analytic near-integrable Hamiltonian theorem \citep{poschel}. Its threshold depends on the chosen complex widths, frequency-domain margins, twist and inverse-twist bounds, and $\gamma_D,\tau_D$. No fixed numerical threshold is asserted here. The resulting tori describe selected bounded motions; they do not imply attraction of arbitrary initial conditions or clearance of the entire flexible beam.
+
+\subsection{Persistence for the actual numerical map}
+\label{kam:map-section}
+
+The continuous theorem does not automatically certify a time-stepping map. Let $\Psi_\Delta$ be the exact implicit map of \eqref{struct:midpoint} or \eqref{struct:central4}, applied in physical balanced coordinates to the relevant autonomous Hamiltonian. The map to analyze in the normal-form chart is
+$\mathcal T_s^{-1}\circ\Psi_\Delta\circ\mathcal T_s$.
+It is generally different from applying midpoint after a nonlinear coordinate transformation. On common, suitably reduced analytic domains, local consistency and the flow perturbation estimate give
+\begin{equation}\label{kam:map-closeness}
+ \norm{\mathcal T_s^{-1}\Psi_\Delta\mathcal T_s
+            -\mathcal R_\Delta^s}
+ \le C_1\Delta\epsilon_s+C_2\Delta^{p+1},
+\end{equation}
+where $\mathcal R_\Delta^s(I,\phi)=(I,\phi+\Delta\omega_s(I))$ and $p=2$ for midpoint or $p=4$ for the fourth-order central scheme. The constants require analytic derivative and chart bounds on those domains.
+
+Exact symplecticity allows a single-valued mixed generating function for the conjugated map. Denote its perturbation relative to $\Delta H_{\rm NF}^s$ by $G_{s,\Delta}$. The same domain reduction gives
+\begin{equation}\label{kam:map-generator-budget}
+ \norm{G_{s,\Delta}/\Delta}
+       \le C_3\epsilon_s+C_4\Delta^p.
+\end{equation}
+The relevant discrete nonresonance condition is
+\begin{equation}\label{kam:map-divisors}
+ \left|e^{\mathrm i\Delta k\cdot\omega}-1\right|
+       \ge\Delta\gamma_D|k|_1^{-\tau_D},
+       \qquad k\ne0.
+\end{equation}
+It includes resonances with integer multiples of $2\pi$, so continuous nonresonance alone does not establish it for a chosen step and frequency.
+
+\begin{proposition}[Conditional persistence for the central maps]
+\label{kam:map-theorem}
+Assume the conservative, analytic, domain and twist premises above, and a unique analytic branch of the exact implicit map. If the discrete Diophantine and frequency-boundary conditions of the analytic map theorem hold and the generator budget \eqref{kam:map-generator-budget} satisfies its smallness condition, that map has a Cantor family of invariant quasiperiodic tori. One applicable path is \citet[Theorem~2, Remark~2 and Section~4]{shang}, using $\tau_D>3$ for two degrees of freedom. The threshold and the constants in \eqref{kam:map-generator-budget} must be verified together on the same domains.
+\end{proposition}
+
+Supplement~\ref{ss:map-kam} records the corresponding small-twist inequality. The budget contains both learning/normal-form error and numerical truncation: decreasing $\Delta$ reduces the latter while leaving $\epsilon_s$ fixed. It does not justify identifying the numerical tori with the continuous ones or transplanting a previously computed torus at different parameters. Floating-point roundoff and a finite Newton residual are separate implementation errors; generic such errors need not define an exact symplectic map.
+
+\subsection{What is established by the present computation}
+\label{kam:scope}
+
+The new-parameter computation supplies the physical and modal inputs, the shared fitted coefficients, finite-data error diagnostics, the algebraic quartic twist and the local structure of the exact midpoint map. It does not supply a validated complex normal-form remainder, an independently certified observation-defect bound or a complete numerical evaluation of either KAM threshold. Accordingly, Propositions~\ref{kam:flow-theorem} and \ref{kam:map-theorem} are conditional conclusions for this benchmark. A finite-amplitude torus could instead be certified through an a posteriori theorem using an approximate embedding, its complex-strip invariance defect, a Diophantine frequency and validated torsion and domain bounds \citep{haro2019aposteriori}; these quantities would need a new calculation for the present parameters. Route~B tracking and the full-beam actuation and clearance analysis remain separate consequences of their stated control and mechanical assumptions.
