+\subsection{Birkhoff structure and a time-dependent canonical representation}
+\label{struct:birkhoff-section}
+
+The mechanical model distinguishes conservative energy from externally supplied and dissipated power. This distinction also determines the geometric structure of its discretization. For a phase vector $z=(q,p)\in\R^{2n}$, a Birkhoff representation uses a one-form $\mathcal R(z,t)^{\mathsf T}\dd z$ and a scalar $\mathcal B$:
+\begin{equation}\label{struct:birkhoff}
+\begin{aligned}
+ \mathcal K_{ij}&=\partial_{z_i}\mathcal R_j-
+                  \partial_{z_j}\mathcal R_i,\\
+ \mathcal K\dot z&=\nabla_z\mathcal B+\partial_t\mathcal R.
+\end{aligned}
+\end{equation}
+Here $\mathcal K$ is a nonsingular, closed, skew-symmetric $2n\times2n$ matrix. It is distinct from the $n\times n$ mechanical mass matrix. The one-form and scalar must satisfy the same differential identities; they cannot be selected independently to absorb an arbitrary dissipative force \citep{su2007}. The endpoint generating-function construction of \citet{feng} and its Birkhoff formulation \citep{fengqin} apply once this compatible structure has been specified.
+
+For the proportional momentum damping $\dot p=-H_q-\alpha(t)p$, define $a(t)=\exp\!\int_{t_0}^{t}\alpha(s)\dd s$. A compatible choice is
+\begin{equation}\label{struct:weighted-hamiltonian}
+\begin{aligned}
+ \mathcal R&=(a p,0),\qquad \mathcal B=aH,
+       \qquad\mathcal K=-aJ,\\
+ \Pi&=a p,\qquad
+ \widetilde H(q,\Pi,t)=a(t)H(q,\Pi/a(t),t).
+\end{aligned}
+\end{equation}
+The equations for $(q,\Pi)$ are canonical. This representation permits a position-dependent mass $M(q)$, provided that all derivatives of $H$ are retained. It is the time dependence of $\widetilde H$, and the transformation back to physical momentum, that carry the damping. Consequently, this construction does not by itself provide the autonomous conservative dynamics needed later for classical KAM theory.
+
+For constant $\alpha$ and the constant-mass benchmark, let $\Delta_t$ denote a physical time step and $r=\exp(\alpha\Delta_t/2)$. Applying implicit midpoint to $\widetilde H$ at the temporal midpoint gives the practical update
+\begin{equation}\label{struct:weighted-midpoint}
+\begin{aligned}
+ q_+-q_-&=\tfrac{\Delta_t}{2}M^{-1}(r p_++r^{-1}p_-),\\
+ r p_+-r^{-1}p_-&=-\Delta_t\nabla V\bigl((q_++q_-)/2\bigr).
+\end{aligned}
+\end{equation}
+Its exact implicit map satisfies
+$(D\Phi)^{\mathsf T}J D\Phi=e^{-\alpha\Delta_t}J$ in physical coordinates, or equivalently preserves the time-dependent Birkhoff two-form between the two endpoints. Ordinary midpoint applied directly to the damped vector field is also second order, but generally has a different geometric identity. The matrix dimensions, endpoint projection and noncommuting-matrix elimination are given in Supplement~\ref{ss:birkhoff}--\ref{ss:matrix}.
+
+\subsection{Central generating functions and the two numerical schemes}
+\label{struct:schemes}
+
+For an autonomous Hamiltonian $h$ in the balanced variables $x=(X,Y)$ and dimensionless time $\tau$, write $f=J\nabla h$ and use $\Delta$ for a time step. A central generating function $S_\Delta$ defines the method
+\begin{equation}\label{struct:central-map}
+ x_+-x_-=J\nabla S_\Delta(m),\qquad m=(x_++x_-)/2.
+\end{equation}
+The choice $S_\Delta=\Delta h$ gives the implicit midpoint method,
+\begin{equation}\label{struct:midpoint}
+ x_+-x_-=\Delta J\nabla h(m).
+\end{equation}
+Whenever the implicit equation has a unique local branch, its Jacobian is the Cayley transform
+\begin{equation}\label{struct:cayley}
+ D\Psi_\Delta=(I-C_\Delta)^{-1}(I+C_\Delta),\qquad
+ C_\Delta=\tfrac12J S_\Delta''(m).
+\end{equation}
+Symmetry of $S_\Delta''$ yields symplecticity. In addition, with $\lambda=Y^{\mathsf T}\dd X$, the central relation gives the single-valued primitive
+\begin{equation}\label{struct:exact-symplectic}
+ \Psi_\Delta^*\lambda-\lambda
+ =\dd\bigl[\bar Y^{\mathsf T}(X_+-X_-)-S_\Delta(m)\bigr].
+\end{equation}
+Thus the map is \emph{exact symplectic}, the property used in the map-KAM argument. This statement concerns the map defined by the exactly solved equations; finite nonlinear-solver residuals are assessed separately.
+
+The fourth-order central scheme is retained as a higher-order extension of the same construction \citep{feng}. Its generating function is
+\begin{equation}\label{struct:central4}
+ S_\Delta^{[4]}(x)=\Delta h(x)
+       +\frac{\Delta^3}{24}f(x)^{\mathsf T}h''(x)f(x).
+\end{equation}
+The Hessian term is necessary: a midpoint evaluation of $\Delta h$ alone remains second order. A Taylor expansion at the temporal midpoint and elimination of that midpoint in favor of the endpoint average give local error $O(\Delta^5)$ for \eqref{struct:central4}; Supplement~\ref{ss:central4} gives the derivation and a harmonic-oscillator check. Both methods have exact symplectic implicit maps on the autonomous Hamiltonian branch. All new-parameter closed-loop test trajectories in Section~\ref{sec:analysis} use ordinary implicit midpoint on the actual vector field:
+\begin{equation}\label{struct:general-midpoint}
+ x_+-x_-=\Delta f_{\rm cl}(m,\tau_m).
+\end{equation}
+All damping, feedback and reference quantities are evaluated consistently at the midpoint; a simultaneously integrated reference is included in the augmented state. Equation~\eqref{struct:midpoint} is the conservative specialization of \eqref{struct:general-midpoint}, not the complete update for a dissipative or tracking loop. Independent comparison trajectories use the reference solvers specified in Section~\ref{sec:analysis}. The fourth-order formula remains a theoretical extension for the present parameter set.
+
+\subsection{The CHNN model class and the four Hamiltonians}
+\label{struct:objects-section}
+
+The complex variables in \eqref{mech:complex-chart} organize the Hamiltonian decoder; the trainable object remains a real energy on the real physical state space. A general CHNN may represent that energy by complex hidden layers followed by a real readout, and may use a separate nonnegative dissipation branch. The force and power equations are then those in \eqref{mech:decoder}--\eqref{mech:power}. The energy and dissipation readouts have different roles: combining their values into one complex output does not make the dissipative dynamics Hamiltonian. Analyticity of the energy on a specified complex neighborhood is an additional requirement for the KAM analysis in Section~\ref{sec:kam}.
+
+To make the identification, force realization and error estimates explicit, the present implementation fixes the known kinetic energy and learns a potential residual. With $\mathsf N=\diag(\nu_1,\nu_2)$ and $\vartheta=d^{\mathsf T}X$, define
+\begin{equation}\label{struct:objects}
+\begin{aligned}
+ g(s)&=1-\cos s-s^2/2,\\
+ V_0(X)&=\tfrac12X^{\mathsf T}\mathsf N X+g(d^{\mathsf T}X),\\
+ h_0(X,Y)&=\tfrac12Y^{\mathsf T}\mathsf N Y+V_0(X),\\
+ h_*&=h_0+R_*,\qquad \widehat h=h_0+\widehat R,\\
+ \delta R&=R_*-\widehat R,\qquad h_c=h_0+\delta R.
+\end{aligned}
+\end{equation}
+The four energies are, respectively, the nominal model, the true conservative model within the declared model class, the learned model and the conservative part of the Route~A closed loop. They have the same quadratic kinetic energy but different potentials. The actual damped, actuated plant is
+\begin{equation}\label{struct:plant}
+ X'=\mathsf N Y,\qquad
+ Y'=-\nabla(V_0+R_*)(X)-\gamma Y+U.
+\end{equation}
+An unknown mass, contact force or input gain is not represented by $R_*(X)$; including such effects requires a corresponding model and observation defect.
+
+A finite analytic readout makes the general CHNN architecture directly auditable: $\widehat R(X)=\sum_{j=1}^{m}\widehat\beta_jF_j(X)$ with frozen real analytic features. The minimal experiment takes $m=1$, $F_1=g(d^{\mathsf T}X)$ and an artificial true coefficient $\beta_*=0.2$. This coefficient is an experimental perturbation, not a measured material property. The architecture can retain a complex implementation of these features, but the experiment tests this constrained readout rather than unrestricted hidden-layer training. Supplement~\ref{ss:full-features} gives the full $35$-coefficient quartic readout and explains the additional control requirements when momentum-dependent terms are learned.
+
+\subsection{Joint integral identification of energy and damping}
+\label{struct:identification-section}
+
+State observations are divided into disjoint training and validation trajectories. For each observation window $[a_\ell,b_\ell]$, let $c_\ell=(c_{\ell X},c_{\ell Y})$ be an endpoint-matching reconstruction and let $U_\ell^{\rm data}$ be the known input. Set $\mathcal V_F=[\nabla F_1,\ldots,\nabla F_m]$. Integrating the momentum equation gives
+\begin{equation}\label{struct:integral-design}
+\begin{aligned}
+ y_\ell={}&c_{\ell Y}(b_\ell)-c_{\ell Y}(a_\ell)\\
+   &+\int_{a_\ell}^{b_\ell}
+      [\nabla V_0(c_{\ell X})-U_\ell^{\rm data}]\dd\tau,\\
+ D_\ell={}&-\int_{a_\ell}^{b_\ell}
+          [\mathcal V_F(c_{\ell X}),c_{\ell Y}]\dd\tau,\\
+ \widehat\theta={}&(\widehat\beta_1,\ldots,\widehat\beta_m,
+                      \widehat\gamma)^{\mathsf T}.
+\end{aligned}
+\end{equation}
+The stacked design has $nL_I$ rows and $m+1$ columns; here $n=2$, and $L_I$ denotes the number of windows. A QR or SVD solve fits $D\theta\simeq y$ and diagnoses excitation through its singular values. Enforcing $\widehat\gamma\ge0$ gives a passive physical damping estimate. The reproduced calculation uses the unconstrained linear solve, so a negative value at roundoff scale in the zero-damping scenario is reported as a numerical fit result rather than physical negative damping.
+
+No acceleration labels are required. Nevertheless, endpoint noise, interpolation, quadrature and unmodeled dynamics still affect the integrated equation. They enter an independently bounded observation defect, not an inferred consequence of a small training loss. Section~\ref{sec:kam} converts that defect and the Gram lower bound into coefficient, force and analytic energy bounds. Both control routes use the same frozen fit and the same physical input map \eqref{mech:modal-ports}.
+
+\subsection{Route A: compensation toward the nominal dynamics}
+\label{struct:route-a-section}
+
+Route~A cancels the estimated damping and potential residual while retaining the coupled nominal magnetic energy:
+\begin{equation}\label{struct:route-a-control}
+ U_A=\widehat\gamma Y+\nabla\widehat R(X).
+\end{equation}
+Substitution into \eqref{struct:plant}, with $\delta\gamma=\gamma-\widehat\gamma$, yields
+\begin{equation}\label{struct:route-a-closed}
+ x'=J\nabla h_c(x)+\binom{0}{-\delta\gamma Y}.
+\end{equation}
+Thus a nonzero conservative learning error changes the closed-loop Hamiltonian to $h_c$; it does not destroy Hamiltonian structure. A nonzero damping error leaves a nonconservative term, with power $h_c'=-\delta\gamma Y^{\mathsf T}\mathsf N Y$. Classical flow or map KAM therefore concerns this physical closed loop only when damping is canceled exactly and the commanded generalized force is realized exactly, or when any remaining force is independently shown to be an autonomous potential gradient and included in $h_c$. A small generic force error is insufficient for this structural premise.
+
+In the exact-compensation limit the closed loop is $h_0$. The nominal reference in the Route~A experiments is the flow of $h_0$ from the same initial state. The oracle uses $U=\gamma Y+\nabla R_*$ solely to separate integration error from identification error; the learned controller uses only the fitted quantities.
+
+\subsection{Route B: tracking a learned conservative reference}
+\label{struct:route-b-section}
+
+Route~B instead generates a reference by $X_r'=\mathsf N Y_r$, $Y_r'=-\nabla\widehat V(X_r)$, where $\widehat V=V_0+\widehat R$. With $e_X=X-X_r$ and $e_Y=Y-Y_r$, use
+\begin{equation}\label{struct:route-b-control}
+\begin{aligned}
+ U_B={}&\widehat\gamma Y+\nabla\widehat V(X)
+                     -\nabla\widehat V(X_r)\\
+       &-\kappa_p\mathsf N^{-1}e_X-\kappa_de_Y,
+       \qquad\kappa_p,\kappa_d>0.
+\end{aligned}
+\end{equation}
+The error satisfies the exact equations
+\begin{equation}\label{struct:route-b-error}
+\begin{gathered}
+\begin{aligned}
+ e_X'&=\mathsf N e_Y,\\
+ e_Y'&=-\kappa_p\mathsf N^{-1}e_X-\kappa_de_Y
+           \\&\quad-\nabla\delta R(X)-\delta\gamma Y,
+\end{aligned}\\
+\begin{aligned}
+ &e_X''+\kappa_de_X'+\kappa_pe_X\\
+ &\qquad=-\mathsf N[\nabla\delta R(X)+\delta\gamma Y].
+\end{aligned}
+\end{gathered}
+\end{equation}
+The homogeneous dynamics are exponentially stable. On a declared domain where the force error and velocity are bounded, convolution with this stable system gives a finite tracking bound; the critically damped formula is in Supplement~\ref{ss:tracking}. Nonzero residual forcing gives a nonzero error envelope. Exact cancellation gives asymptotic tracking and preserves a zero initial tracking error.
+
+For the synthetic truth-only comparator, define $V_*=V_0+R_*$ and replace only the plant compensation terms:
+\begin{equation}\label{struct:route-b-oracle}
+\begin{aligned}
+ U_B^{\rm or}={}&\gamma Y+\nabla V_*(X)
+                         -\nabla\widehat V(X_r)\\
+              &-\kappa_p\mathsf N^{-1}e_X-\kappa_de_Y.
+\end{aligned}
+\end{equation}
+This oracle retains the same learned reference $(X_r,Y_r)$ as \eqref{struct:route-b-control}; it does not replace that reference by the true conservative flow. Substitution gives the homogeneous error equation $e_X''+\kappa_de_X'+\kappa_pe_X=0$. Its midpoint update is the Cayley transform of the constant error-system matrix, providing the numerical oracle check in Section~\ref{sec:analysis}. The implemented learned feedback remains \eqref{struct:route-b-control} and does not read $R_*$ or $\gamma$.
+
+KAM analysis in this route concerns $\widehat h$ and hence the existence of selected quasiperiodic references. The feedback system itself includes damping and a time-dependent reference. Its tracking guarantee comes from \eqref{struct:route-b-error}, not from symplecticity of that closed loop. Section~\ref{sec:application} connects both $U_A$ and $U_B$ to physical magnetic force and torque, with calibration requirements and full-beam conditions stated separately.
